WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.958
<v Examiner>Genetics, the fine print.

2
00:00:02.958 --> 00:00:07.106
<v Examiner>The one-mark facts her quiz keys ask that no written answer covers.

3
00:00:07.556 --> 00:00:13.042
Genetics is the biggest pile of marks in her Module 3 quizzes, and most of it is closed:

4
00:00:13.042 --> 00:00:16.657
one-mark multiple choice, drop-downs, and matching tables.

5
00:00:16.657 --> 00:00:19.026
The written tracks give you the ideas.

6
00:00:19.026 --> 00:00:22.891
This one gives you the small facts, in the words her keys use,

7
00:00:22.891 --> 00:00:25.945
so nothing in a closed question catches you cold.

8
00:00:26.395 --> 00:00:29.406
The hook: a line through means they've died.

9
00:00:29.406 --> 00:00:31.321
Y-linked is fathers to sons.

10
00:00:31.321 --> 00:00:34.400
Outside the square, gametes; inside, zygotes.

11
00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:36.315
And one trait is monohybrid.

12
00:00:36.765 --> 00:00:37.869
Reading her charts.

13
00:00:37.869 --> 00:00:41.123
Beyond squares, circles and shading, three more symbols.

14
00:00:41.123 --> 00:00:44.028
A line through a symbol means the person has died.

15
00:00:44.028 --> 00:00:48.212
A horizontal line joining a man and a woman is a couple that reproduces.

16
00:00:48.212 --> 00:00:52.802
And their children hang from one line below them, which marks them as siblings.

17
00:00:52.802 --> 00:00:57.334
The generations are numbered down the side in Roman numerals, one, two, three,

18
00:00:57.334 --> 00:01:01.169
and the people in each generation are numbered from left to right.

19
00:01:01.169 --> 00:01:03.784
So two, one means generation two, person one.

20
00:01:03.784 --> 00:01:08.490
Many of her pedigree questions are just counting off the chart: how many females,

21
00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:11.453
how many have died, how many children a couple had.

22
00:01:11.903 --> 00:01:15.182
One pattern the written tracks don't name: Y-linked.

23
00:01:15.182 --> 00:01:20.667
The gene sits on the Y chromosome, so only males have it, it shows in every generation,

24
00:01:20.667 --> 00:01:23.315
and a father passes it to all of his sons.

25
00:01:23.315 --> 00:01:28.863
Her drop-down: this condition is Y-linked, since it occurs in males in every generation,

26
00:01:28.863 --> 00:01:31.637
and sex-linked, since it affects only males.

27
00:01:32.087 --> 00:01:35.613
Her haemophilia genogram is Queen Victoria's family.

28
00:01:35.613 --> 00:01:37.918
Haemophilia is X-linked recessive.

29
00:01:37.918 --> 00:01:43.004
Victoria was a carrier, and her son Leopold had haemophilia: X little h, Y.

30
00:01:43.004 --> 00:01:48.225
And when her charts put a star on an allele, the star marks the affected one.

31
00:01:48.225 --> 00:01:50.327
X star, Y, is an affected male.

32
00:01:51.777 --> 00:01:53.030
Her one-mark words.

33
00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:56.063
A trait: a characteristic determined by genes.

34
00:01:56.063 --> 00:02:00.217
A mutagen: an agent that brings about a change in the DNA code.

35
00:02:00.217 --> 00:02:04.107
Haploid is the chromosome number of a gamete, twenty-three.

36
00:02:04.107 --> 00:02:07.272
Diploid is the number in a body cell, forty-six.

37
00:02:07.272 --> 00:02:10.701
Chromosome pair twenty-three is the sex chromosomes,

38
00:02:10.701 --> 00:02:15.251
and the X and Y chromosomes decide the sex: that's sex determination.

39
00:02:15.251 --> 00:02:17.691
Her karyotype wording, word for word:

40
00:02:17.691 --> 00:02:22.241
a complete diploid complement display of homologous chromosome pairs.

41
00:02:22.241 --> 00:02:26.593
On her normal male karyotype, you call it male because it has a Y.

42
00:02:27.043 --> 00:02:28.673
Mitochondrial DNA, her way.

43
00:02:28.673 --> 00:02:33.502
It's part of the genome, but not part of the nuclear DNA: it's extrachromosomal.

44
00:02:33.502 --> 00:02:36.219
Mitochondria divide on their own in the cell,

45
00:02:36.219 --> 00:02:40.385
and the egg's mitochondria pass from a mother to all of her children.

46
00:02:40.385 --> 00:02:42.679
So on her chart of an affected mother,

47
00:02:42.679 --> 00:02:47.630
the grandchildren who escape it are the ones whose mothers didn't have the defect.

48
00:02:47.630 --> 00:02:50.829
And genes passed from the mother only: mitochondrial.

49
00:02:51.279 --> 00:02:53.596
Two of her keys to learn as written.

50
00:02:53.596 --> 00:02:59.067
Most human traits are determined by multiple alleles: that's her key, in two quizzes,

51
00:02:59.067 --> 00:02:59.775
so pick it.

52
00:02:59.775 --> 00:03:02.028
And her Huntington's true or false:

53
00:03:02.028 --> 00:03:07.756
the extra C A G repeats make a toxic protein with a long run of the amino acid glutamine,

54
00:03:07.756 --> 00:03:09.945
a glutamine stretch called poly Q,

55
00:03:09.945 --> 00:03:13.614
which progressively breaks down nerve cells in the brain.

56
00:03:13.614 --> 00:03:13.935
True.

57
00:03:15.385 --> 00:03:16.165
The crosses.

58
00:03:16.165 --> 00:03:21.755
A cross of one trait is a monohybrid cross, and every cross in her bank is monohybrid.

59
00:03:21.755 --> 00:03:24.744
Dihybrid only ever turns up as a wrong option.

60
00:03:24.744 --> 00:03:29.034
On a Punnett square, the letters outside are the possible gametes,

61
00:03:29.034 --> 00:03:32.153
and the letters inside are the possible zygotes.

62
00:03:32.603 --> 00:03:34.774
Her deaf-dog problem is a test cross.

63
00:03:34.774 --> 00:03:39.704
A dog that can hear is big D big D or big D little d, and you can't tell by looking.

64
00:03:39.704 --> 00:03:42.931
So you cross him with a deaf female, little d little d.

65
00:03:42.931 --> 00:03:46.511
If he's big D big D, every puppy is big D little d and hears.

66
00:03:46.511 --> 00:03:50.559
If he's big D little d, half the puppies are big D little d and hear,

67
00:03:50.559 --> 00:03:52.965
and half are little d little d, and deaf.

68
00:03:52.965 --> 00:03:55.899
So a single deaf puppy proves he's big D little d.

69
00:03:56.349 --> 00:03:57.064
Her flowers.

70
00:03:57.064 --> 00:04:00.936
Red crossed with white gives pink: incomplete dominance, a blend.

71
00:04:00.936 --> 00:04:06.060
Red crossed with white gives flowers with red and white parts: codominance, both show.

72
00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:11.243
Her wavy-haired child, from a curly allele and a straight allele: incomplete dominance.

73
00:04:11.243 --> 00:04:14.221
And sickle cell, her incomplete dominance example.

74
00:04:14.221 --> 00:04:19.285
Two parents who are both big A little a have a twenty-five per cent chance of a child

75
00:04:19.285 --> 00:04:21.668
with the lethal form, little a little a.

76
00:04:21.668 --> 00:04:26.493
If one parent is big A little a and the other is big A big A, the chance is zero.

77
00:04:26.943 --> 00:04:30.649
Her polygenic drop-down: polygenic, or multifactorial,

78
00:04:30.649 --> 00:04:34.424
inheritance shows continuous variation in a population,

79
00:04:34.424 --> 00:04:39.022
and a trait like height or skin colour is determined by many genes,

80
00:04:39.022 --> 00:04:41.493
each with a small effect on its own.

81
00:04:42.943 --> 00:04:45.423
Her word problems, and the catch in each.

82
00:04:45.423 --> 00:04:48.751
Freckles are dominant, and neither parent has freckles.

83
00:04:48.751 --> 00:04:53.227
So both are little f little f, and the chance of a freckled child is zero.

84
00:04:53.227 --> 00:04:58.067
Her false statement: because the freckle allele is dominant, it can easily hide.

85
00:04:58.067 --> 00:04:59.822
A dominant allele can't hide.

86
00:04:59.822 --> 00:05:02.847
If either parent carried it, they'd have freckles.

87
00:05:03.297 --> 00:05:06.750
Two parents with widow's peaks have a child without one.

88
00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:08.107
That's not infidelity.

89
00:05:08.107 --> 00:05:12.239
Both parents are heterozygous, each passes on the recessive allele,

90
00:05:12.239 --> 00:05:14.768
and that's a twenty-five per cent chance.

91
00:05:14.768 --> 00:05:20.257
The same goes for a carrier father whose son has P K U: the mother must be a carrier too.

92
00:05:20.707 --> 00:05:21.996
Her P T C question.

93
00:05:21.996 --> 00:05:26.136
About eighty per cent of people can taste the chemical P T C.

94
00:05:26.136 --> 00:05:31.565
Her key says true: the higher frequency suggests the tasting allele is dominant.

95
00:05:32.015 --> 00:05:33.666
And her two fetal tests.

96
00:05:33.666 --> 00:05:37.450
The most common form of fetal testing is amniocentesis.

97
00:05:37.450 --> 00:05:39.238
But in her matching table,

98
00:05:39.238 --> 00:05:44.604
the most reliable test for genetic abnormalities is chorionic villus sampling.

99
00:05:44.604 --> 00:05:48.250
Read which one she's asking for: common, or reliable.

100
00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:49.414
The traps.

101
00:05:49.414 --> 00:05:52.558
Y-linked is males only, father to every son.

102
00:05:52.558 --> 00:05:58.417
Polygenic is many genes; multiple alleles is one gene with more than two versions.

103
00:05:58.417 --> 00:06:02.204
And outside the square is gametes, inside is zygotes.

104
00:06:02.654 --> 00:06:03.244
<v Examiner>Once more.

105
00:06:03.244 --> 00:06:04.659
<v Examiner>The genetics fine print.

106
00:06:07.109 --> 00:06:09.345
A line through a symbol: deceased.

107
00:06:09.345 --> 00:06:11.186
A horizontal line: a couple.

108
00:06:11.186 --> 00:06:14.211
Their children hang from one line as siblings.

109
00:06:14.211 --> 00:06:18.288
Generations are Roman numerals, people numbered left to right.

110
00:06:18.288 --> 00:06:22.234
Y-linked: only males, every generation, father to every son.

111
00:06:22.234 --> 00:06:25.653
Victoria was a carrier, and Leopold had haemophilia.

112
00:06:25.653 --> 00:06:31.177
A trait is a characteristic determined by genes, and a mutagen changes the DNA code.

113
00:06:31.177 --> 00:06:37.030
Haploid is twenty-three, diploid forty-six, and pair twenty-three is the sex chromosomes.

114
00:06:37.030 --> 00:06:41.304
Mitochondrial DNA is part of the genome, but not the nuclear DNA.

115
00:06:41.304 --> 00:06:45.381
Her key: most human traits are determined by multiple alleles.

116
00:06:45.831 --> 00:06:47.900
A one-trait cross is monohybrid.

117
00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:51.327
Outside the Punnett square, gametes; inside, zygotes.

118
00:06:51.327 --> 00:06:54.495
A test cross uses a homozygous recessive partner,

119
00:06:54.495 --> 00:06:58.504
and one recessive offspring proves the parent is heterozygous.

120
00:06:58.504 --> 00:07:04.064
Red and white giving pink is incomplete dominance; red and white parts is codominance.

121
00:07:04.064 --> 00:07:09.172
Two sickle cell carriers have a twenty-five per cent chance of the lethal form.

122
00:07:09.172 --> 00:07:12.276
Polygenic: many genes, each with a small effect.

123
00:07:12.276 --> 00:07:15.702
Two non-freckled parents can't have a freckled child.

124
00:07:15.702 --> 00:07:18.547
The most common fetal test is amniocentesis;

125
00:07:18.547 --> 00:07:21.586
her most reliable is chorionic villus sampling.
